  "predictive_services_discussion": "Above normal temperatures will persist across the southern Plains into the Lower Mississippi Valley today, but light southerly flow off the Gulf will bring additional low level moisture with minimum relative humidity forecast to remain above 35%. In addition, isolated showers and thunderstorms in the afternoon and evening will produce localized wetting rain. Drier conditions with minimum relative humidity of 25-35% will persist in the Southeast. In the West, abundant moisture from Hurricane Polo will move into the Southwest with widespread showers, scattered thunderstorms, and localized flooding. Scattered showers and thunderstorms are also likely to develop farther to the north and east into the central Rockies and much of the central and northern Plains. Farther west, dry conditions are expected for California, the western Great Basin, Northwest, and northern Rockies, amid below normal temperatures. Rain and wind will diminish in the Northeast today, with drier conditions and moderate trade winds in Hawai’i. National Predictive Services Outlook (7-Day Significant Fire Potential Outlook) National Weather Service, Storm Prediction Center: Fire Weather Outlook Map and discussion National Weather Service: Fire Weather Hazards Map National Weather Service, Weather Prediction Center: National Forecast Chart and Short Range Forecast Discussion 6 Minutes for Safety topics can be found here.",
